Subject: Proposed new feature for TMB: simulating the fixed and/or random effects when simulate() is called.
Message-ID: <5tWDRj__OU0.kTbkWyasAAAJ@tmb-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/tmb-users/c/5tWDRj__OU0/m/kTbkWyasAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The SIMULATE section of TMB can be used to simulate the observed
data, making simulation analysis convenient. The function can be called with a
set of parameters including the fixed and random effects otherwise it uses the
previous estimates by default. Some diagnostics also involve simulating the
fixed and/or random effects. This is done in VAST by using the function rmvnorm_prec(),
which takes the MLEs and the jointPrecision from the standard deviation report and
randomly samples the fixed and random effects from a multivariate normal, and
passes these to the simulate() function call. It would be useful to integrate
this functionality directly in the TMB simulate() function with an augment to
determine if the random, fixed and random, or no parameters are simulated. In this way it could be used in any application without rewriting the code.

I am sure there may be some technical issue or possible
improvements that would need to be considered.

